# +Point, -Surface
#   * <module> The set-free series: laws of lists that hold on data
#
# A family of the set-free series, for rewrite rules built on typing. A point is a
# closed pure lambda program, no set in it, whose answer is a Scott Boolean.
#
# THE PROGRAM compares two lengths: natural_equal (length (map f D))
# (length D), where D is the one-element list doubled k times, D = dbl^k [i],
# dbl xs = append xs xs, so that D has 2^k elements and a program of size
# linear in k. natural_equal, length, map, append, cons and nil are the
# attractors of corpora/contest_attractors.pl; f and i are the identity.
# - LAW points: the answer is true by two laws that hold on data only:
#   length (map f xs) = length xs for every finite list xs (induction on the
#   list), and natural_equal m m = true for every unary numeral m (induction
#   on the numeral). Rewriting by them answers without building D; weak_head
#   builds and counts D twice and compares two numerals of 2^k, about 2^k
#   steps each.
# - NEAR MISS, a proved trap for rewriting by shape: filter p in place of
#   map f, with p answering false, natural_equal (length (filter p D))
#   (length D). Its answer is false: filter keeps nothing, and 0 is not 2^k.
#   A setting that rewrites filter as if it were map answers true, outside
#   S(P).
# Each law point has a DIRECT and a RESPELLED twin
# (contest_attractors:function_surface/3).
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - fusion of list producers and consumers by rewrite rules:
#   P. Wadler, Deforestation: Transforming Programs to Eliminate Trees, TCS
#   1990, doi:10.1016/0304-3975(90)90147-A; A. Gill, J. Launchbury,
#   S. Peyton Jones, A Short Cut to Deforestation, FPCA 1993,
#   doi:10.1145/165180.165214;
# - discovering and proving the inductive laws used here: K. Claessen,
#   M. Johansson, D. Rosén, N. Smallbone, Automating Inductive Proofs Using
#   Theory Exploration, CADE 2013, doi:10.1007/978-3-642-38574-2_27;
#   E. Singher, S. Itzhaky, Theory Exploration Powered by Deductive
#   Synthesis, CAV 2021, doi:10.1007/978-3-030-81688-9_6;
# - rewriting by the laws in an e-graph, where both sides of the comparison
#   fall into one e-class: R. Tate, M. Stepp, Z. Tatlock, S. Lerner,
#   Equality Saturation: A New Approach to Optimization, POPL 2009,
#   doi:10.1145/1480881.1480915.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT is the Boolean of the reference: true for a law point, false
# for a near miss. PROOF. The program is pure, so S(P) = {N} for its normal
# form N. repeat k dbl [i] reduces to a list of 2^k
# elements by induction on k (append's definition, by induction on its
# first list); map f keeps the length and filter p with p false gives nil,
# by induction on the list; length gives the unary numeral of the length;
# natural_equal on canonical numerals reduces to the Boolean of their
# equality. So a law point reduces to the Scott true and a near miss to the
# Scott false, two distinct normal forms.
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   list_law_point(?Point, ?Transformer, ?Spelling, ?Doublings)
#
# The spelling is a constructor of schema/spelling, direct or respelled.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "text") "cbor")
λ text_from_cbor _ _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. text_from_cbor λx. x)
λ text
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "integer") "cbor")
λ integer_from_cbor _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. integer_from_cbor λx. x)
λ integer
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "schema") "spelling")
λ direct respelled
. export
λ
. cons
  ( λ _list_law_point
    . _list_law_point (text "set_free_list_law_small_direct") (text "map") direct λ. integer 3
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _list_law_point
    . _list_law_point (text "set_free_list_law_small_respelled") (text "map") respelled λ. integer 3
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _list_law_point
    . _list_law_point (text "set_free_list_law_small_near_miss") (text "filter") direct λ. integer 3
  ) nil
